  "result": "Invoices now have a `currency` field, and it worked when I ran it. Nothing is needed from you.\n\n- **Old invoices:** a new step, `10_invoice_currency`, fills in each invoice's currency from its account. This step changes the saved data; such steps are called migrations.\n- **New invoices:** they get the account's currency when they are made.\n- **Showing it:** `GET /invoices/:id` returns `\"currency\":\"EUR\"`, and `show` prints a `Currency` line.\n- **Fixed a hidden bug:** migrations ran in text order, so `10` would have run before `2` on a new, empty database. They now run in number order. Migrations 1–9 were not edited, so production's saved records of them still match.\n- **Check:** I set up a database on migration 9 with the old code. Then I ran the new code. It ran only migration 10. `show inv_1` and the HTTP call both showed `EUR`. All 21 tests pass.\n\nOld code ignores the new field, so rolling back is safe. The work is one commit, `9852415`, on a new local branch `invoice-currency`. I did not push it.",
